## What Happened

Apple removed DSA support from its native crypto libraries (Security.framework / CryptoKit). .NET on macOS relies on those native APIs, so `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. Linux and Windows still work because OpenSSL and Windows CNG continue to support DSA.

This follows the same pattern as the earlier .NET 7 breaking change where several legacy algorithms were dropped on macOS.

## How to Fix

You have a few options, depending on your constraints:

1. **Use OpenSSL on macOS** — Install OpenSSL via Homebrew and P/Invoke into it, or use a managed wrapper. This keeps your existing verification logic mostly intact.

2. **Use BouncyCastle** — The `BouncyCastle.Cryptography` NuGet package has a fully managed DSA implementation that works on all platforms:

   ```csharp
   // Install: dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
   using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
   using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

   var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
   verifier.Init(false, publicKeyParams);
   verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
   bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
   ```

3. **Migrate signatures to a modern algorithm** (best long-term) — If you control the signing side, re-sign with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**, both of which are supported everywhere in modern .NET.

## Recommendation

If you only need to *verify* existing legacy signatures and can't re-sign, option **2 (BouncyCastle)** is the simplest cross-platform fix with no native dependencies. If you can migrate, move to ECDSA/Ed25519 and retire DSA entirely.
